-- title:
--   Adjoining a root of a distinguished monic polynomial
-- statement:
--   Let $S$ be a commutative ring which is local and Noetherian and which is complete and separated for the $\mathfrak m_S$-adic filtration, where $\mathfrak m_S$ denotes the maximal ideal of $S$. Let $g \in S[X]$ be monic with $\deg g \ge 1$ and suppose every coefficient $g_i$ with $i < \deg g$ lies in $\mathfrak m_S$, i.e. $g$ is distinguished. The assertion is that $A := S[X]/(g)$, formed as `AdjoinRoot g`, carries a structure of local ring for which, writing $\mathfrak m_A$ for its maximal ideal: $A$ is complete and separated for the $\mathfrak m_A$-adic filtration; $A$ is Noetherian; $A$ is a finite $S$-module; the composite of the structure map $S \to A$ with the residue map $A \to A/\mathfrak m_A$ is surjective, so that $A$ has the same residue field as $S$; the canonical root $x =$ `AdjoinRoot.root g` lies in $\mathfrak m_A$; and $$\mathfrak m_A = \mathfrak m_S A + (x),$$ the sum of the image of $\mathfrak m_S$ under $S \to A$ and the ideal generated by $x$. The local ring structure is produced as part of the existential statement, the remaining assertions being stated for it.
--
--   This is the standard fact that adjoining a root of a distinguished (Eisenstein-type) monic polynomial to a complete Noetherian local ring again yields a complete Noetherian local ring, module-finite over the base and with unchanged residue field, together with the explicit description of its maximal ideal. It is used to recognise rings presented by such equations — in particular those arising from Igusa-type presentations of moduli of elliptic curves with level structure — as objects of the category of complete local algebras over the base.
-- source:
--   https://github.com/anthropics/fermats-last-theorem/blob/aa2d8b34692b16c70f699536de0d8e75b9a3e9ef/Theorems/Thm_AdjoinRoot_exists_isLocalRing_isAdicComplete_of_monic_of_coeff_mem_maximalIdeal.lean

import Mathlib

